Authoro
Public Provenance Registry for Authorship and Creative Work
Working domain: authoro.net Working product family: Authoro Registry, Authoro Proof, Authoro Mark, Authoro API, Authoro MCP Status: Concept PRD Core thesis: Authoro does not need to observe every work being created. It becomes the neutral public registry where trusted systems submit evidence about how a work was created.

1. Product Summary
Authoro is a public provenance registry for creative works.
Its job is not to decide whether something is “AI” or “human.”
Its job is to answer:
What evidence exists about who created this work, how it was created, when it was created, what tools participated, and whether the evidence can be verified?
Any compatible editor, recorder, publisher, AI system, authorship-verification service, institution, or individual can submit structured evidence to Authoro.
Authoro normalizes that evidence into a canonical Proof Record.
Every registered work receives:
* a permanent Authoro ID
* a canonical public proof URL
* a cryptographically bound document fingerprint
* one or more signed provenance attestations
* an Authoro Proof Mark that can be embedded beside the published work
Example:
authoro.net/p/AU-7K3F92
A reader clicking the Authoro Mark sees the evidence behind that specific version of the work.
Authoro therefore acts less like an editor and more like a combination of:
* DOI registry
* SSL certificate authority/resolver
* provenance ledger
* Creative Commons-style recognizable mark
* trust infrastructure for authorship

2. Problem
Generative AI has made content-origin questions increasingly difficult.
Existing systems generally fall into four categories.
AI detectors
Attempt to infer whether text was AI-generated from the final artifact.
Problem:
Inference is probabilistic and can produce false positives and negatives.
Writing-process recorders
Record keystrokes, revisions, pastes, writing sessions, and related telemetry.
Problem:
Each platform produces its own isolated evidence.
Publisher/editor histories
Google Docs, Word, CMS platforms, and other editors maintain revision information.
Problem:
The history usually stays inside the originating platform.
Provenance standards
Systems such as C2PA provide formats for carrying signed provenance information.
Problem:
Ordinary readers still need a recognizable place to resolve and inspect provenance.
There is no obvious consumer destination equivalent to:
“Click here to see the evidence behind this work.”
Authoro intends to become that destination.

3. Product Principle
Authoro should verify claims rather than manufacture certainty.
The product must avoid statements such as:
“This document was definitely written by a human.”
Instead it should display claims such as:
Continuous composition was observed by Provider X.
Provider Y reported 92% manually entered text.
Publisher Z confirmed publication on this date.
The final publication matches the registered SHA-256 fingerprint.
The author self-attested that generative AI was used for editing only.
Authoro records who asserted what and whether the assertion itself can be authenticated.

