8. Privacy
Raw creation history should not be public by default.
Authors need control.
Two rules govern every visibility and disclosure choice:
* Visibility can decrease disclosure, but never erase provenance.
* Minimal hides content, not verifiability.
Public visibility presets:
Minimal
Shows:
* work
* author
* date
* providers: every issuer stays named ("Reported by X"), and author-supplied claims stay labelled "Author supplied"
* document fingerprint and integrity status
* verification and documentation links
* correction, revocation, withdrawal and dispute status
Hides:
* raw envelope details
* detailed telemetry and statistics
* the author's note
* the raw event history
Minimal preserves verification links by design. Privacy can hide content; it should not hide provenance. Minimal means less exposed detail, not less verifiability: hiding the underlying links would turn the record into "trust the badge" rather than "inspect the evidence".
Minimal also withholds the hidden content from the public API, but keeps the verification primitives: each evidence object's hash, issuer, schema, signature metadata, timestamps and verification status. A reader can confirm that a registered evidence object is unchanged without learning its private contents.
Two kinds of links:
* Documentation links: public issuer documentation, methodology pages, registry records and source attestations. Always visible, under every preset.
* Private evidence links: links that expose private drafts, replay data, deleted text or personally sensitive information. These obey the visibility preset.
Until links can be told apart (V1), every link is a documentation link and stays visible.
A Minimal record might show, per source:
Creation Process Observed
Reported by Writermark: continuous composition observed
✓ Signature valid (once issuer keys exist; until then "Unverified")
[View source evidence]
"View source evidence" opens an Authoro evidence page, never a third-party URL directly. Under Minimal it is sparse but still proves that the evidence object exists, who reported it, when, and that it is unchanged. Under fuller presets it shows the evidence itself. From there, the source's own links (issuer documentation, methodology, original provider record) are offered.
Standard
Adds:
* session count
* manual/paste/AI proportions
* high-level timeline
* evidence methods
* the author's note, AI uses and tools
Detailed
Adds:
* expanded event statistics
* provider detail
* version history
Full Replay
Author explicitly publishes revision replay/history.
Authoro should never assume deletion history is public.
Changing the preset of a record that has been public is itself recorded in the record's public history.

